          h2 id="heslo" 
            | Структура словникової статті
            a href="#heslo" onclick="showhide ('help3');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Натисніть, щоб переглянути версію мовою International Sign"
            div id="help3" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/helpIS03.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide ('help3');"
          p Кожна словникова стаття складається з трьох частин:
          ul
            li Заголовок
            li Формальна частина (граматичні відомості тощо)
            li Семантична частина
            li Словосполучення
            
          h3 id="header"
            | Заголовок
            a href="#header" onclick="showhide ('help4');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Натисніть, щоб переглянути версію мовою International Sign"
            div id="help4" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/helpIS04.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
               div.close onclick="showhide ('help4');"
          p Заголовок статті для <strong>усної мови</strong> містить саме заголовне слово (лему) (1), нестандартну вимову в дужках (2) і, за наявності, інший варіант написання (3).
          img src="/img/help/headerwriteEN.png" align="left"
          p Заголовок статті для <strong>жестової мови</strong> містить відео та транскрипції, оскільки для жестових мов не існує усталеної загальноприйнятої системи письма. Основною формою, що представляє лему, є відео з фронтального ракурсу (1); до нього додається відео збоку (2). Також наводиться транскрипція системою SignWriting (3), а за потреби — системою HamNoSys (4).
          p 
            em Оскільки жестомовці можуть бути лівшами або правшами, інформацію про провідну руку наведено у верхньому лівому куті. (L — лівша, R — правша) (5)
            img src="/img/help/headersignEN.png" align="left"
          
          h3 id="formal"
            | Формальна частина
            a href="#formal" onclick="showhide ('help5');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Натисніть, щоб переглянути версію мовою International Sign"
            div id="help5" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/helpIS05.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
          p Формальна частина леми в <strong>усній мові</strong> може містити такі відомості:
          ul
            li Походження слова (для запозичень),
            li Частина мови,
            li Можливі варіанти (написання, частина мови та морфологічні форми),
            li Морфологічна парадигма змінюваних частин мови (таблиця відмінювання),
            li Стилістичні особливості (територіальні, соціальні або стилістичні обмеження у вживанні виразу).
            
          p Формальна частина леми в <strong>жестовій мові</strong> може містити такі відомості:
          ul
            li Походження жесту,
            li Частина мови,
            li Мовленнєвий компонент — указується лише для лексем, у яких він обов’язковий під час артикуляції; його записують малими літерами. Частини слова в дужках є необов’язковими. Якщо можливі кілька варіантів компонента, їх розділяють скісною рискою. Наприклад: «term(ín)/datum».
            li Усний компонент — зазначається лише тоді, коли він обов’язковий,
            li Граматичні або стилістичні варіанти,
            li Територія поширення (місцевість, де лексема зазвичай уживається, або типова група користувачів)
            
          h3 id="semantic"
            | Семантична частина
            a href="#semantic" onclick="showhide ('help6');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Натисніть, щоб переглянути версію мовою International Sign"
            div id="help6" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/helpIS06.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
          p Ця частина містить пояснення значення з прикладами, відомості про семантичні зв’язки та сталі багатослівні вирази, до складу яких входить описуваний вираз.
          p <strong>Пояснення значення</strong> (семантична дефініція) загалом відповідає звичній формі словникових визначень. Для жестових мов передбачено незначні зміни, що допомагають зберегти природність висловлювання <em>(наприклад, вираз у заголовку та вираз, що становить основу пояснення, не обов’язково мають належати до тієї самої частини мови; на початку дефініції можна повторити описуваний вираз)</em>.
          p In accordance with lexicographic tradition all meanings which relate to one form are indicated under one lemma. In the dictionary a distinction is made between homonymy <em>(multiple lemmas)</em> and polysemy <em>(only one lemma)</em>; unclear cases are classified under one lemma. Moreover, the various interpretations of the meaning may be indicated by one of the <strong>semantic areas</strong>., which provides information about the context <em>(scientific discipline or sector of human activity in general)</em> in which the given expression in the mentioned meaning usually can occur. Such indication is taken over from the original sources in cases where also the original definition is taken over; for newly created lemmas <em>(especially lemmas of a terminological nature)</em>, one of the semantic areas mentioned in the used sources is assigned (for more detailed information on sources see page "About"). Meanings indicated for specific areas follow after the general meanings (i.e. after meanings which do not belong to a specific area). A complete list of used semantic areas can be viewed here.
          p <strong>Приклади вживання</strong> подано у формі речень. Вони демонструють граматичні <em>(або стилістичні)</em> особливості та специфічні конотації описуваної леми. Іноді приклади насамперед показують типові синтаксичні зв’язки <em>(наприклад, зі звичними дієсловами чи прийменниками)</em>, а іноді уточнюють семантичну дефініцію. Приклади беруть із різних джерел: для жестових мов їх створюють, перевіряють або схвалюють у робочих групах носії національної жестової мови; для усних мов приклади а) знаходять у корпусах текстів (наприклад, за допомогою Sketch Engine* для чеської мови) або б) беруть зі словників-джерел і переформульовують у речення, якщо в корпусі не знаходять вдалих прикладів.
          p Further information in this field are the <strong>semantic relations</strong> to the units of the given language (<strong>synonyms, antonyms</strong> and to the units of the other language in the dictionary (<strong>semantic equivalents</strong> <em>/also called translations/ and vice versa</em>). Items in this section of the semantic part of the lemma may contain a reference to another separate lemma <em>(if exists in target language dictionary)</em>, or have the form of plain text.
          
          h3 id="colloc" 
            | Стійкі словосполучення
            a href="#colloc" onclick="showhide ('help7');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Натисніть, щоб переглянути версію мовою International Sign"
            div id="help7" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/helpIS07.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
          p Завершальний розділ статті містить сталі сполучення з описуваною лемою. Це можуть бути як короткі сполучення (фразеологізми, ідіоми, деякі порівняння), так і повні прислів’я та приказки. До словника включають багатослівні одиниці з ідіоматичним значенням, тобто такі, загальне значення яких не можна вивести лише зі значень окремих слів.

          h2 id="lexcat"
            | Categories of Lexical Units in Czech Sign Language
          p The first piece of information provided in the formal description of an entry in Czech Sign Language is the lexical category of the sign. As in any natural language, Czech Sign Language distinguishes groups of expressions that exhibit certain shared morphological, syntactic (distributional), and semantic characteristics. Across languages, these systematic similarities (and differences) are traditionally captured by classifying them into lexical categories known as parts of speech. Defining individual lexical categories based on a combination of morphological, syntactic, and semantic properties of lexical units is thus considered a sufficiently general tool for systematising the vocabulary of any natural language. In line with this tradition, each lexical unit in the dictionary is typically assigned one lexical category. Given the grammatical structure of sign languages in general and the degree of theoretical analysis of Czech Sign Language in particular, classification into lexical categories is not a straightforward process. Therefore, it is necessary to briefly comment on the approach we use and define the terms employed in the dictionary.
          p Our strategy is to apply insights from general linguistics on the one hand and the current research on Czech Sign Language on the other. We are open to applying traditional lexical categories where the material of Czech Sign Language allows for classification according to these categories. In other cases, we use terms from scholarly works on Czech Sign Language or those used in international literature. Given that a comprehensive theoretical description of the grammar of Czech Sign Language is still under development at relevant academic institutions, the classification we offer does not aim higher than at providing a starting point for further academic discussion.
          p The traditional parts of speech we use to describe the lexicon of Czech Sign Language are: noun, verb, pronoun, numeral, conjunction, and particle. From the scholarly works on Czech Sign Language, we also accept the category known as the "categorising expression." From the international tradition, we adopt the terms classifier, size and shape specifier, modifier, and wh-word, which we translate as "classifier," "shape and size specifier," "modifier," and "interrogative expression."
          p Below are the general criteria and typical grammatical features of individual categories. Exceptions to these are listed in the grammatical note of the lexeme to which they apply.
          p Criteria for Assigning Signs to Lexical Categories

          h3 
            | 1) Nouns 
            a href="#noun" onclick="showhide('noun');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="noun" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISnoun.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide('noun');"          
          p Signs classified as nouns meet the following criteria:
          ul 
            li <strong>Semantic</strong>: Denote entities (person, animal, object...) or qualities, materials, etc.
            li <strong>Syntactic</strong>: a) They can be combined with basic numerals and other quantifiers; b) The addition of another noun is always interpreted coordinately.
            li <strong>Morphological</strong>: Reduplication of the sign, or incorporation of a numeral or reduplication of a proform, expresses plural. A supporting criterion is the occurrence of verbal components with nouns, in contrast to the presence of oral components with verbs.
          p Within nouns, two subcategories are distinguished: common and proper nouns. Common nouns denote groups of entities (classes of persons, objects, phenomena, etc.). Proper nouns, or name signs, refer to a specific entity, distinguishing it from others of the same kind and identifying it as unique within a given class.

          h3 
            | 2) Verbs
            a href="#verb" onclick="showhide('verb');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Český znakový jazyk"
            div id="verb" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISverb.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide('verb');"            
          p Signs are classified as verbs if they meet the following defined criteria:
          ul 
            li <strong>Semantic</strong> criteria: They denote actions or states.
            li <strong>Syntactic</strong> criteria: They do not combine with basic numerals.
          p <strong>Morphological</strong> criteria:
          ul 
            li Show various types of agreement (e.g., person, number, or location).
            li Aspectual modification is possible through non-manual means.
            li Reduplication is used to indicate aspect (or, in some cases, agreement with an object in terms of number).
          p Additionally, auxiliary indicators include the presence of oral components (in contrast to the verbal components seen in nouns) or the possibility of negation using non-manual means.

          p 
            strong Types of Verbs
          p Based on further criteria, verbs are divided into the following four types:
          ul 
            li 
              strong Plain Verbs
              p These are usually articulated on or near the body (e.g., RÁD [LIKE]). They do not take affixes for person, place, or number. Some may take aspectual affixes. The subject and object must be expressed lexically, typically using pronouns. The subject precedes the verb, and the object follows it.
            li 
              strong Agreement Verbs
              p These verbs take affixes for person, number, and aspect but not for location (e.g., VRÁTIT [RETURN]).
              p Agreement verbs are further divided into four subtypes:
              ul 
                li <strong>Subjective</strong>: Movement starts and ends to express the subject (e.g., VRÁTIT).
                li <strong>Objective</strong>: Movement start and end express the object and subject, respectively (e.g., POZVAT [INVITE]).
                li <strong>Restricted</strong>: These lack full agreement with all objects and subjects, often excluding agreement with the first person (e.g., UZNAT [ACKNOWLEDGE]).
                li <strong>Reciprocal</strong>: These express mutual actions between two subjects, with each hand corresponding to a subject (e.g., SEZNÁMIT [INTRODUCE]).
            li
              strong Spatial Verbs
              p These take location affixes but not affixes for person, number, or aspect (e.g., PSÁT [WRITE]). They can indicate the movement of an object through space, specify a location, or describe a medium.
            li
              strong Classifier Verbs
              p These incorporate handshapes (classifier forms) that agree with the object’s properties. Agreement is based on the object's shape, size, or other characteristics.

          h3 
            | 3) Pronouns
            a href="#pron" onclick="showhide('pron');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="pron" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISpron.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide('pron');"          
          p Pronouns include exclusively expressions with deictic or anaphoric functions. They can be further divided into demonstrative pronouns and possessive pronouns:
          ul  
            li <strong>Demonstrative</strong> pronouns refer to a participant in the communication or an object within the communicative context (deictic use) or to a sign (anaphoric use) towards which an extended index finger is directed (e.g., I) or additional fingers for plural forms (e.g., we two).
            li <strong>Possessive </strong>pronouns indicate belonging to a participant in the communication or a sign towards which the speaker’s palm is directed (e.g., his).

          h3 
            | 4) Numerals
            a href="#num" onclick="showhide('num');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="num" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISnum.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide('num');"          
          p Numerals include expressions related to numbers and counting. Based on semantic and other criteria, they are categorized as basic, ordinal, multiplicative, iconic, and indefinite numerals:
          ul
            li <strong>Basic</strong> numerals specify a simple, definite number and are signed in neutral space.
            li <strong>Ordinal</strong> numerals express order and exhibit richer inflectional morphology, such as shifts in signing space or orientation changes.
            li <strong>Multiplicative</strong> numerals denote the frequency or repetition of actions, or the degree of a property exceeded in comparison.
            li <strong>Iconic</strong> numerals mimic the written numeral’s form through movement during signing, either partially or fully.
            li <strong>Indefinite</strong> numerals represent an indeterminate number.

          h3 
            | 5) Modifiers
            a href="#mod" onclick="showhide('mod');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="mod" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISmod.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide('mod');"          
          p Modifiers include signs that provide additional specification to entities or verbal actions. They are categorized into qualitative, spatial, temporal, and aspectual modifiers:
          ul 
            li <strong>Qualitative</strong> modifiers express the qualities of entities or actions (e.g., beautiful). They differ from other groups in that they are typically gradable.
            li <strong>Temporal</strong> modifiers adjust the timing of the action in the statement and can act as a temporal framework (e.g., today). They differ by incorporating numerical morphemes, with potential reduplication indicating frequency rather than plurality.
            li <strong>Aspectual</strong> modifiers adjust the action in terms of its initiation, conclusion, progression, or absence of realization (e.g., already). These are exclusively associated with verbs.
            li <strong>Spatial</strong> modifiers are generally deictic expressions that indicate not only the location of an object in space but also, potentially, the trajectory of its movement (e.g., beside).

          h3 
            | 6) Conjunctions
            a href="#con" onclick="showhide ('con');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="con" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexIScon.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide ('con');"          
          p Conjunctions include signs that express various logical relationships between two sentences or sentence elements.

          h3 
            | 7) Interrogative Expressions
            a href="#inter" onclick="showhide ('inter');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="inter" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISiterr.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide ('inter');"          
          p Interrogative expressions correspond to the linguistic concept of wh-words. These are signs used to form content questions, eliciting specific information.

          h3 
            | 8) Categorization Expressions
            a href="#cat" onclick="showhide ('cat');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="cat" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexIScat.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false
              div.close onclick="showhide ('cat');"          
          p The class of categorization expressions is defined by enumeration. From a semantic perspective, these are more or less grammaticalized expressions with the ability to organize hypernym-hyponym relationships among lexemes, specifically distinguishing the subordinate or superordinate level from the basic level.

          h3 
            | 9) Particles
          p Particles include expressions that convey the speaker's subjective attitude toward the statement, its immediacy, or the degree of certainty. Within this group, negative particles are a distinct type. They negate a sentence element or the entire sentence (e.g., AT ALL).

          h3 
            | 10) Classifiers
            a href="#clf" onclick="showhide ('clf');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Click to show International Signing"
            div id="clf" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISclf.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false 
              div.close onclick="showhide ('clf');"          
          p Classifiers include classifier handshapes and shape/size specifiers.
          p For classifier handshapes, we distinguish the following semantic types:
          ul 
            li <strong>Object</strong> classifiers (e.g., FIGURE).
            li <strong>Body</strong> part classifiers (e.g., CLF-5o).
            li <strong>Handling</strong> classifiers (e.g., CLF-AS).
            li <strong>Contact</strong> classifiers (e.g., CLF-A1).
            li <strong>Shape</strong> and size specifiers provide more specific details about an object's form (e.g., a noun). They describe attributes such as thickness, width, or outline, including depth, thickness, width, contour, surface texture, or quantity of objects (e.g., SQUARE).

          h2 id="word-order" 
            | Basic word order in Czech Sign Language
            a href="#sentence" onclick="showhide ('sentence');"
              img src="/img/sl.png" style="width: 32px; vertical-align: bottom;" title="Český znakový jazyk"
            div id="sentence" style="display:none; position:fixed; top:105px; right:10px;"
              video preload="metadata" src="https://files.dictio.info/help/lexISsentence.mp4" height="300" controls=true autoplay=false 
              div.close onclick="showhide ('sentence');"

          p The first position in a sentence is occupied by the so-called time frame, followed by the indication of place, then the subject and the object. The verb occupies the last position in the sentence.
          p Within the time frame, a more general term is placed first, followed by a more specific one. For example, "April 18" is expressed as APRIL 18, and "at eight in the morning" as MORNING EIGHT-O'CLOCK.
          p When expressing a place, the so-called spiral principle is used, proceeding from the general to the specific. For example, the information "in the square in Královo Pole in Brno" would be expressed as: CZECH REPUBLIC, BRNO, KRÁLOVO POLE, SQUARE.
          p When expressing the subject and object (generally within noun phrases), the noun is placed first, followed by its modifiers. The typical order of elements within noun phrases referring to people is as follows: a) noun, pronoun b) noun, modifier.
          p The typical order of elements within a complex object (or subject) is as follows: noun, modifier or specifier expressing property or shape, modifier expressing color. For example, "a nice black CRT television" would be expressed as: TELEVISION CRT BLACK NICE. Individual elements of the noun phrase can be omitted, but their order must be maintained. For example, "a small red car" is expressed as CAR SMALL RED, or "a nice table" as TABLE NICE, or "a square table" as TABLE SQUARE.
          p The verb depends on the subject (noun) with which it agrees. This agreement is reflected, for instance, in the corresponding classifier. When two verbs appear in sequence (typically a modal and a main verb), the modal verb comes first. For example, WANT SWIM. Verbs can be modified and intensified using non-manual components.



